{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense collapses because it concedes the Miller-Tichý refutation destroys the formal basis for 'verisimilitude' (closeness to truth), yet simultaneously relies on the intuitive notion that a surviving theory has 'higher empirical content' and is the 'best available target.' Without a coherent definition of verisimilitude or progress toward truth, the preference for the surviving theory becomes indistinguishable from the very inductive habit Popper rejected: assuming that because a theory worked in the past (survived tests), it is 'better' for future action. The target attempts to replace 'truth approximation' with 'error elimination,' but fails to explain why eliminating error in the past makes a theory practically superior for future prediction unless one assumes the future resembles the past (induction). Thus, the claim that the preference is 'non-inductive' is internally inconsistent given the admitted loss of the theoretical framework (verisimilitude) that was supposed to ground the distinction.","premise":"If the Miller-Tichý refutation implies that we cannot even rationally claim a falsified theory is 'further from the truth' than a surviving one, then the basis for preferring the survivor collapses into arbitrary convention.","successor_question":"Can Popperian methodology define 'progress' or 'preferability' without implicitly relying on an inductive assumption that the future will resemble the past, once the formal concept of verisimilitude is removed?","target":"7021dbe4e8cc3f444b21b2ea28b224fb84c9a5d240e587196da3fbdf841c2d00"}]}